var a=[{title:`What low impact means`,text:`Low-impact movements usually keep at least one foot on the ground, or use equipment such as a stationary bike to take out ground impact. Low impact can still be high intensity and can still load your joints. A hard cycling interval can push your heart and lungs, and some step exercises still ask a lot of your knees and hips.`},{title:`Good movement choices`,text:`Brisk walking, marching, step touches, shadow boxing without bouncing, controlled step-ups, cycling and elliptical machines are all practical options. In a small room, alternate brisk marching with deliberate side steps or light punches. Pick movements that feel stable, and do not add fast twists just to make it harder.`},{title:`Example`,text:`After a gradual warm-up, repeat 45 seconds of brisk marching and 45 seconds of easy walking six times. Set your own pace and rest; do not copy someone else’s speed. To progress, lengthen the active periods or the whole session before you go for explosive speed.`},{title:`Match the reason you are avoiding impact`,text:`If the issue is noise, dropping jumps may be enough. If a knee, ankle or back is sensitive, even ordinary stepping can hurt, and you may need to see a doctor or physiotherapist. Low-impact training is not a treatment and does not guarantee pain relief.`},{title:`Using TrainUp`,text:`In Profile, set whether jumping is OK, how much noise is OK, your surface, your level, your equipment and any limitations by body area. The plan uses all of them together, and when jumping is off it leaves out jump rope and other jumping drills. Marching or cycling can keep the effort up in their place. Rate the session’s effort afterward as usual.`}];var s=[{label:`CDC adult physical activity recommendations`,url:`https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/guidelines/adults.html`},{label:`CDC measuring activity intensity and the talk test`,url:`https://www.cdc.gov/physicalactivity/basics/measuring/index.html`},{label:`ACE exercise library`,url:`https://www.acefitness.org/resources/everyone/exercise-library/`}];var i={sections:a,sources:s};export{i as default,a as sections,s as sources};